# Engineering quality

Simple, professional changes. No spaghetti, no duplicate systems, no speculative abstractions, no "AI slop."

## Before implementation

- Inspect the existing implementation in the files you will touch (and their callers).
- Search for existing helpers, components, hooks, and utilities before adding new ones.
- Understand the root problem; do not patch symptoms in isolation.
- Prefer the **smallest correct solution** that fits current architecture.
- Reuse established patterns (routes, Prisma access, UI tokens, shared components).
- Do not introduce abstractions for hypothetical future needs.

## During implementation

- Keep changes scoped to the assigned task; avoid unrelated refactors.
- Do not duplicate logic or run parallel implementations of the same capability.
- Preserve strong TypeScript typing; avoid `any` unless truly unavoidable.
- Do not suppress type or lint errors to make checks pass.
- Do not add dependencies unless the task clearly requires them.
- Prefer readable, boring code over clever code.
- Keep functions and components focused; avoid premature abstraction and premature optimization.
- Do not change application behavior unless the task requires it.

## Before completion

- Remove debug code and dead code introduced by your change.
- Inspect the **complete diff** (not only the last file edited).
- Look for duplicate implementations, unnecessary complexity, security regressions, and performance regressions.
- Run relevant validation (see `.cursor/rules/testing.mdc`); do not claim a check passed unless it actually ran.

## Self-review before opening a PR

Review your diff like a senior engineer. Fix legitimate issues before opening the PR.

Check for:

- Unnecessary complexity
- Duplicate logic
- Poor or premature abstractions
- Scope creep
- Weak typing
- Security issues (auth, tenancy, secrets, untrusted URLs/inputs)
- Performance regressions
- Dead code
- Missing error handling
- Accidental behavior changes

Then open the PR with an honest summary and testing notes. **Do not merge**; stop for human review.
